Here's a clean example showing how to pass props to a child component after an API call resolves in Vue 3 with `<script setup>`:

## Parent Component (`Parent.vue`)

```vue
<script setup>
import { ref, onMounted } from 'vue'
import ChildComponent from './ChildComponent.vue'

const userData = ref(null)
const loading = ref(true)
const error = ref(null)

onMounted(async () => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/user')
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch')
    userData.value = await response.json()
  } catch (e) {
    error.value = e.message
  } finally {
    loading.value = false
  }
})
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <p v-if="loading">Loading...</p>
    <p v-else-if="error">Error: {{ error }}</p>
    <!-- Only render child when data is available -->
    <ChildComponent v-else :user="userData" />
  </div>
</template>
```

## Child Component (`ChildComponent.vue`)

```vue
<script setup>
import { computed } from 'vue'

const props = defineProps({
  user: {
    type: Object,
    required: true
  }
})

const displayName = computed(() => {
  return `${props.user.firstName} ${props.user.lastName}`
})
</script>

<template>
  <div class="child">
    <h2>Welcome, {{ displayName }}</h2>
    <p>Email: {{ user.email }}</p>
  </div>
</template>
```

## Key Points

| Pattern | Why |
|---|---|
| `v-else` on child | Prevents rendering child before data exists |
| `required: true` on prop | Enforces that the prop must be provided |
| `ref(null)` initial state | Clean "no data yet" state |
| `onMounted` for fetch | Runs once after component is in DOM |

